#!/usr/bin/env bash
# doctor-artifact: $HOME/.mesh/.tamper-state 1800 phone
# doctor-vocab: $HOME/.mesh/.tamper-state 4:ACTIVE,RECENT,STALE,COLD,NEVER
#   field 4 is classify_recency's BAND, this sense's own vocabulary — not a verdict. Its STALE
#   (30min-2h, "historical, body has been at rest") is LESS concerning than COLD, which does not
#   alarm, so a content scan reading it as a fault is inverted. mesh-dash blanks the field only on
#   an exact match against this list, so a new band or a real alarm word here still reds.
# mesh-tamper — the body's TAMPER/disturbance sense. Watches the phone's SIGNIFICANT_MOTION
# hardware trigger over a window: it fires ONLY on real physical motion (the Android sensor HAL
# decides), so for a body that is supposed to sit PARKED, a fire = "someone moved/picked it up."
#
# Why distinct from mesh-body-motion: body-motion SAMPLES the pose at an instant (it can miss a
# brief disturbance between samples). mesh-tamper WATCHES the event stream for the whole window —
# it catches a quick bump even if the phone is back at rest by the time you'd next sample.
#
#   mesh-tamper                watch ~8s → [body-moved] (fired) or [body-quiet] (no motion)
#   mesh-tamper --window N     watch N seconds (default 8)
#   mesh-tamper --edge         emit ONLY on [body-moved] (silent when quiet — cron/stream-friendly)
#   mesh-tamper --status       non-blocking cached read: last verdict + age (no phone watch)
#                              — makes the sense OBSERVABLE (state artifact ~/.mesh/.tamper-state)
#   mesh-tamper --test         classify-logic fixtures (offline) + deps + phone-reachability gate
#                              (exit 2 = phone unreachable; classify fixtures run first regardless)
#
# Read-only (no actuators). Honest-organ: phone-unreachable → exit 2, never a fabricated "quiet".
# A "quiet" is only valid if the watch actually ran — an unreachable phone is NOT "quiet".
#
# PROOF-THE-WATCH-RAN (fidelity fix 2026-06-25): a SIGNIFICANT_MOTION watch that does NOT fire is
# killed by the inner `timeout`, which returns 124 — the SAME code the OUTER ssh timeout returns
# when the phone is unreachable. The old code treated any rc=124 as "unreachable", so a genuinely
# STILL body (watch ran, nothing fired) was misreported n/a instead of [body-moved] — i.e.
# [body-quiet] was unreachable in practice and the sense could never assert a confident still.
# Fix: the remote command emits a completion sentinel `TAMPER_EOF=<rc>` AFTER termux-sensor. The
# sentinel prints ONLY if the ssh session ran the remote shell to completion (phone reachable).
# The decision is now SENTINEL-gated, not rc-gated:
#   * sentinel PRESENT  → the watch RAN (phone reachable): "values" blob → [body-moved]; else [body-quiet]
#   * sentinel ABSENT   → ssh never completed the session → unreachable → exit 2 (never a fake quiet)
# Robust to termux timeout semantics (blocks-until-fire OR returns-empty): either way a reachable
# still body yields sentinel+no-values → [body-quiet], and a dead link yields no sentinel → exit 2.
# The moved verdict and the unreachable verdict are UNCHANGED; this only recovers [body-quiet].
#
# EVENT-TIME ATTRIBUTION (fidelity 2026-07-07, additive): a disturbance event now records WHO-context
# AT THE MOMENT IT FIRES — the cached mesh-presence familiarity (freshness-gated, ≤900s) classified
# with mesh-tamper-attrib's exact vocabulary: ATTRIBUTED (familiar person nearby) · SUSPECT
# (empty/appliance-only/stranger) · AMBIGUOUS (transitional/inconclusive) · UNKNOWN (presence
# stale/missing — honest, never a fabricated benign). Why here and not only in mesh-tamper-attrib:
# the attrib cron (*/10) fuses CURRENT cached states, so a moved verdict overwritten by a quiet
# before the next attrib run is never attributed, and the recency window (ACTIVE/RECENT, 30 min)
# that consumers alarm on (mesh-window-state ALERT → "security concern") carries no attribution at
# all — every routine familiar pickup reads as a security concern for its whole 30-min window.
# Event-time capture is also strictly better evidence: presence AT the fire, not minutes later.
#   * event log line: <epoch>|<attrib>  (legacy bare-epoch lines still parse; their attrib=UNKNOWN)
#   * state field 6:  last_attrib — attribution of the newest recorded event (NONE if no events),
#     so a recency consumer can distinguish "RECENT + ATTRIBUTED" (familiar handling) from
#     "RECENT + SUSPECT/UNKNOWN" (genuinely unattributed) from the state file alone.
#   * [body-moved] line gains `attrib=<verdict>`; [body-quiet]/--status gain `last_attrib=<verdict>`.
# All additive: markers, recency=, events_24h= and fields 1-5 are byte-compatible; 5-field IFS
# readers ignore field 6. mesh-tamper-attrib stays the CURRENT-state fusion (+ --live); this is the
# PER-EVENT record it cannot reconstruct after the fact.
#
# BURST DETECTION (fidelity, additive): recency (when) and events_24h (how many, total) can't tell
# a single incidental bump (bag jostled once) from SUSTAINED handling (someone actively rummaging —
# multiple SIGNIFICANT_MOTION fires packed into a few minutes). classify_burst() counts events in a
# short BURST_WINDOW (default 10min) and labels BURST (>= BURST_FLOOR, default 3) / ISOLATED (1-2) /
# NONE (0). BURST is a materially stronger signal than an isolated fire and is surfaced independent
# of ambient context (it's self-evidencing, not a household-clock read) — it also STACKS with an
# ANOMALOUS quiet-hour ambient read for the strongest combined severity this sense can express.
#   * state field 7: burst — BURST|ISOLATED|NONE, computed at write time from the event log.
#   * [body-moved]/[body-quiet]/--status lines gain `burst=<label>`.
# All additive: fields 1-6 unchanged; 6-field IFS readers ignore field 7.
#
# CADENCE / PERIODICITY (fidelity, additive): a BURST counts fires but is blind to their RHYTHM. A
# phone resting on a VIBRATING surface (washer spin, a buzzing shelf) fires SIGNIFICANT_MOTION at
# REGULAR gaps; a person RUMMAGING fires at IRREGULAR gaps — same count, opposite meaning.
# classify_cadence() takes the burst-window event epochs and labels the coefficient of variation of
# the inter-event gaps: PERIODIC (CoV ≤ CADENCE_CV_PERMILLE/1000, regular → likely mechanical) /
# ERRATIC (irregular → consistent with human handling) / NA (< 3 events, no rhythm to judge). A
# PERIODIC quiet-hour burst is WEAKER evidence of a person than an ERRATIC one, so a consumer can
# down-rank the mechanical case instead of firing a SUSPECT alarm on a buzzing shelf.
#   * state field 8: cadence — PERIODIC|ERRATIC|NA, computed at write time (NA unless burst==BURST).
#   * [body-moved]/[body-quiet]/--status lines gain `cadence=<label>`; the [body-moved] severity
#     line adds a mechanical-vs-human note when the burst is PERIODIC/ERRATIC.
# All additive: fields 1-7 unchanged; 7-field IFS readers ignore field 8.
set -uo pipefail
export PATH="$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH"
[ -n "${HOME:-}" ] || HOME=$(getent passwd "$(id -u)" 2>/dev/null | cut -d: -f6); export HOME
[ -f "$HOME/.mesh/nodes" ] && . "$HOME/.mesh/nodes"

STATE="$HOME/.mesh/.tamper-state"      # <verdict>|<epoch>|<window>|<recency>|<events_24h>|<last_attrib> — liveness + cached consumer read
EVENT_LOG="$HOME/.mesh/.tamper-events"  # one `<epoch>|<attrib>` line per moved event (legacy bare-epoch OK); pruned to 24h
PRESENCE_STATE="${MESH_TAMPER_PRESENCE_STATE:-$HOME/.mesh/.presence-state}"   # mesh-presence cached summary (key=val line)
PRESENCE_STALE="${MESH_TAMPER_PRESENCE_STALE:-900}"                            # presence cron is */10min → 15min freshness gate
BURST_WINDOW="${MESH_TAMPER_BURST_WINDOW:-600}"                                # 10min — see classify_burst()
BURST_FLOOR="${MESH_TAMPER_BURST_FLOOR:-3}"                                    # events within BURST_WINDOW to call it BURST
CADENCE_CV_PERMILLE="${MESH_TAMPER_CADENCE_CV:-350}"                           # CoV(gaps)*1000 cut; <= → PERIODIC — see classify_cadence()

# classify_tamper <rc> <raw-output> → echoes moved|quiet|unreachable (pure, offline-testable).
# Sentinel-gated so a still body (watch ran, nothing fired) is a confident quiet, NOT a fake n/a.
classify_tamper() {
  local rc="$1" out="$2"
  if printf '%s' "$out" | grep -q 'TAMPER_EOF='; then       # watch ran → phone was reachable
    if printf '%s' "$out" | grep -q '"values"'; then
      echo "moved"
    else
      echo "quiet"
    fi
  else                                                       # ssh session never completed → unreachable
    echo "unreachable"
  fi
}

# classify_recency <now_epoch> <last_event_epoch> → echoes "LABEL|seconds_since"
# Captures the "how long since the body was last tampered with?" signal — distinct from
# the per-watch verdict (moved/quiet) which is just "did anything fire in the last N seconds?".
# Bands (FALSIFIABLE via --test, 5-min/30-min/2-hour thresholds — change them and the tests flip):
#   ACTIVE   last event 0–5 min ago       — fresh tampering, room-mind should be alert
#   RECENT   last event 5–30 min ago      — recent activity, worth a glance
#   STALE    last event 30 min – 2 hours ago — historical, body has been at rest
#   COLD     last event > 2 hours ago    — long quiet, no concern
#   NEVER    no recorded event            — first run, no baseline
classify_recency() {
  local now="$1" last="$2"
  [ -n "$last" ] && [ "$last" -gt 0 ] 2>/dev/null || { echo "NEVER|no recorded event"; return; }
  local dt=$((now - last))
  if   [ "$dt" -lt 300 ];  then echo "ACTIVE|${dt}s ago (within 5min)"; return; fi
  if   [ "$dt" -lt 1800 ]; then echo "RECENT|${dt}s ago (within 30min)"; return; fi
  if   [ "$dt" -lt 7200 ]; then echo "STALE|${dt}s ago (within 2h)"; return; fi
  echo "COLD|${dt}s ago (long quiet)"
}

# classify_burst <count_in_window> → BURST|ISOLATED|NONE. Pure, offline-testable.
# recency/events_24h answer "when" and "how many total" — neither distinguishes ONE incidental
# bump (bag jostled, phone knocked once) from SUSTAINED handling (someone actively searching/
# rummaging: multiple SIGNIFICANT_MOTION fires packed into a few minutes). A burst is materially
# stronger evidence of a person actively engaging the phone right now, not a passive nudge.
#   BURST     >= BURST_FLOOR (default 3) events within BURST_WINDOW (default 10min)
#   ISOLATED  1-2 events within the window — a single/couple of incidental disturbances
#   NONE      0 events within the window — nothing recent enough to judge
# FALSIFIABLE via --test: change BURST_FLOOR/BURST_WINDOW and these thresholds flip.
classify_burst() {
  local n="$1"
  [ -n "$n" ] && [ "$n" -gt 0 ] 2>/dev/null || { echo "NONE"; return; }
  if [ "$n" -ge "$BURST_FLOOR" ] 2>/dev/null; then echo "BURST"; else echo "ISOLATED"; fi
}

# classify_cadence <epoch> [epoch ...] → PERIODIC|ERRATIC|NA. Pure, offline-testable.
# A BURST (>= BURST_FLOOR fires) counts events but is blind to their RHYTHM. Two very different
# sources produce the same count: a rhythmic MECHANICAL disturbance — the phone resting on a
# vibrating surface (washer spin, a buzzing shelf) fires SIGNIFICANT_MOTION at REGULAR intervals —
# vs ERRATIC human handling (rummaging: a flurry of fires at IRREGULAR gaps). A PERIODIC burst is
# materially WEAKER evidence of a person actively engaging the body than an ERRATIC one, so a
# consumer can down-rank a regular-cadence quiet-hour burst instead of alarming on it.
# Method: sort the event epochs, take inter-event gaps, compute the coefficient of variation
# CoV = stddev/mean of the gaps (scale-free — independent of how fast the source fires).
#   PERIODIC  CoV*1000 <= CADENCE_CV_PERMILLE (default 350 → ~0.35): regular, machine-like
#   ERRATIC   CoV above the cut: irregular gaps, consistent with human handling
#   NA        < 3 events (< 2 gaps to judge) OR coincident timestamps (mean gap 0)
# FALSIFIABLE via --test: change CADENCE_CV_PERMILLE and evenly/unevenly spaced fixtures flip.
classify_cadence() {
  [ "$#" -ge 3 ] || { echo "NA"; return; }
  local thr="${MESH_TAMPER_CADENCE_CV:-$CADENCE_CV_PERMILLE}"
  printf '%s\n' "$@" | sort -n | awk -v thr="$thr" '
    /^[0-9-]+$/ { t[++n]=$1 }
    END {
      if (n<3) { print "NA"; exit }
      k=0; m=0
      for (i=2;i<=n;i++){ g[++k]=t[i]-t[i-1]; m+=g[k] }
      m=m/k
      if (m<=0) { print "NA"; exit }                 # coincident/zero-gap events → cannot judge
      v=0; for (i=1;i<=k;i++){ d=g[i]-m; v+=d*d }
      cv=(sqrt(v/k)/m)*1000
      if (cv<=thr) print "PERIODIC"; else print "ERRATIC"
    }'
}

# recent_event_epochs <log_file> <now_epoch> <window_sec> → space-separated event epochs within
# the window (newest-last order preserved from the log). Pure, file-bound — feeds classify_cadence.
recent_event_epochs() {
  local f="$1" now="$2" win="$3" cutoff epoch
  cutoff=$(( now - win ))
  [ -f "$f" ] || return 0
  while IFS= read -r line; do
    [ -z "$line" ] && continue
    epoch="${line%%|*}"
    [ "$epoch" -ge "$cutoff" ] 2>/dev/null && printf '%s ' "$epoch"
  done < "$f"
}

# ── context-gated disturbance severity (discover/tamper-rhythm-severity): a live body disturbance
#    means something DIFFERENT depending on whether the household is active. Fuse mesh-ambient-clock so a
#    SIGNIFICANT_MOTION fire while the house is QUIET (nobody active) reads as ANOMALOUS/suspicious,
#    while the same fire during activity is routine handling. Same pattern as the shipped
#    lan-newdevice-occupancy-severity, applied to tamper. Additive: the [body-moved] marker + the
#    recency/events_24h fields (consumer contract) are UNCHANGED; only a severity phrase is added.
# ambient_context → QUIET | ACTIVE | UNKNOWN, mapping mesh-ambient-clock's OWN labels (QUIET/NIGHT-QUIET
# = nobody active → QUIET; MODERATE/ACTIVE = household active → ACTIVE; DATA-WARMUP/DATA-STALE/absent/
# unparseable → UNKNOWN). Honest-fusion: an unreadable clock renders UNKNOWN (current default line), NEVER
# a faked "active/all-clear" that would suppress a genuinely anomalous quiet-hour disturbance.
ambient_context() {
  [ -n "${MESH_TAMPER_AMBIENT:-}" ] && { _tamper_map_ambient "$MESH_TAMPER_AMBIENT"; return; }   # test/override hook (raw label)
  command -v mesh-ambient-clock >/dev/null 2>&1 || { printf 'UNKNOWN'; return; }
  local lbl
  lbl="$(mesh-ambient-clock --json 2>/dev/null | sed -n 's/.*"label":"\([^"]*\)".*/\1/p' | head -1)"
  _tamper_map_ambient "$lbl"
}
_tamper_map_ambient() {
  case "$1" in
    QUIET|NIGHT-QUIET) printf 'QUIET' ;;
    MODERATE|ACTIVE)   printf 'ACTIVE' ;;
    *)                 printf 'UNKNOWN' ;;
  esac
}
# tamper_sev_render CTX WINDOW EVENTS_24H [ATTRIB] [BURST] → the [body-moved] line with ambient-gated
# disturbance severity + event-time attribution + burst context. recency stays ACTIVE (a live fire);
# ambient UNKNOWN → the prior default phrasing (marker + fields untouched); attrib defaults UNKNOWN;
# burst defaults NONE (a caller passing only 4 args gets the pre-burst line, byte-compatible).
# BURST fires its own note independent of ambient (it's self-contained evidence — sustained physical
# handling right now — not gated on a household-clock read), and STACKS with an ANOMALOUS QUIET
# read: a burst during expected-quiet is the strongest single signal this sense can produce.
tamper_sev_render() {
  local ctx="$1" window="$2" e="$3" attrib="${4:-UNKNOWN}" burst="${5:-NONE}" cadence="${6:-NA}" sev="" note=""
  case "$ctx" in
    QUIET)  sev="(ANOMALOUS — disturbance during expected-quiet) "
            note=" — household ambient QUIET (nobody active): a body disturbance now is anomalous, investigate" ;;
    ACTIVE) sev="(routine) "
            note=" — household ambient ACTIVE: normal handling during activity" ;;
    *)      : ;;   # UNKNOWN → current default line, no faked context
  esac
  if [ "$burst" = "BURST" ]; then
    note="${note} — BURST: ${BURST_FLOOR}+ fires within ${BURST_WINDOW}s, sustained handling not a single bump"
    # Cadence refines a BURST: a REGULAR rhythm points at a mechanical/periodic source (phone on a
    # vibrating surface), materially WEAKER evidence of a person than an ERRATIC (irregular) burst.
    case "$cadence" in
      PERIODIC) note="${note}; cadence=PERIODIC — regular gaps, likely a mechanical/periodic source, NOT human rummaging" ;;
      ERRATIC)  note="${note}; cadence=ERRATIC — irregular gaps, consistent with human handling" ;;
    esac
  fi
  printf '[body-moved] %sSIGNIFICANT_MOTION fired in %ss window — body physically disturbed (recency=ACTIVE events_24h=%s attrib=%s burst=%s cadence=%s)%s\n' \
    "$sev" "$window" "$e" "$attrib" "$burst" "$cadence" "$note"
}

# ── event-time attribution (2026-07-07): WHO-context captured AT the SIGNIFICANT_MOTION fire.
# presence_pf → "<personal_presence>|<familiarity>" from mesh-presence's cached state, freshness-
# gated (≤PRESENCE_STALE). Degraded reads are DISTINCT tokens (MISSING/STALE/UNREADABLE), never a
# fabricated presence — same honest-degraded read as mesh-tamper-attrib's read_presence_cached.
presence_pf() {
  [ -n "${MESH_TAMPER_PRESENCE:-}" ] && { printf '%s' "$MESH_TAMPER_PRESENCE"; return; }   # test/override hook ("pp|fam")
  [ -f "$PRESENCE_STATE" ] || { printf 'MISSING|MISSING'; return; }
  local mtime age line pp fam
  mtime="$(stat -c %Y "$PRESENCE_STATE" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)"
  [ "$mtime" -gt 0 ] || { printf 'MISSING|MISSING'; return; }
  age=$(( $(date +%s) - mtime ))
  [ "$age" -le "$PRESENCE_STALE" ] || { printf 'STALE|STALE'; return; }
  line="$(cat "$PRESENCE_STATE" 2>/dev/null)"
  pp="$(printf '%s' "$line" | grep -oE 'personal_presence=[A-Za-z_]+' | head -1 | cut -d= -f2)"
  fam="$(printf '%s' "$line" | grep -oE 'familiarity=[A-Za-z_]+' | head -1 | cut -d= -f2)"
  printf '%s|%s' "${pp:-UNREADABLE}" "${fam:-UNREADABLE}"
}

# classify_event_attrib <personal_presence> <familiarity> → ATTRIBUTED|SUSPECT|AMBIGUOUS|UNKNOWN.
# Pure, offline-testable. SAME vocabulary + mapping as mesh-tamper-attrib's moved branch
# (cross-sense vocab contract — one attribution language on the mesh, not two):
#   ACTIVE_NEAR/FAR + FAMILIAR → ATTRIBUTED (likely household handling)
#   ACTIVE_NEAR/FAR + STRANGER → SUSPECT    (unfamiliar person accounted nearby)
#   EMPTY / APPLIANCE-only     → SUSPECT    (nobody personal accounted — unattributed)
#   transitional presence      → AMBIGUOUS  (ARRIVING/DEPARTING/INITIAL/AMBIGUOUS)
#   degraded presence input    → UNKNOWN    (stale/missing — honest, never a faked benign)
classify_event_attrib() {
  local pp="$1" fam="$2"
  case "$pp" in
    ACTIVE_NEAR|ACTIVE_FAR)
      case "$fam" in
        FAMILIAR) echo "ATTRIBUTED" ;;
        STRANGER) echo "SUSPECT" ;;
        *)        echo "AMBIGUOUS" ;;
      esac ;;
    EMPTY|APPLIANCE)                      echo "SUSPECT" ;;
    ARRIVING|DEPARTING|INITIAL|AMBIGUOUS) echo "AMBIGUOUS" ;;
    *)                                    echo "UNKNOWN" ;;
  esac
}

# last_event_attrib <log_file> → attribution recorded with the NEWEST event. NONE when no events
# (nothing to attribute — matches mesh-tamper-attrib's quiet verdict); a legacy bare-epoch line
# (pre-attrib log) is an event whose attribution was never captured → UNKNOWN, not a guess.
last_event_attrib() {
  local f="$1" line
  [ -f "$f" ] || { echo "NONE"; return; }
  line="$(tail -n 1 "$f" 2>/dev/null)"
  [ -n "$line" ] || { echo "NONE"; return; }
  case "$line" in
    *'|'*) echo "${line#*|}" ;;
    *)     echo "UNKNOWN" ;;
  esac
}

# count_recent_events <log_file> <now_epoch> <window_sec> → echoes integer count.
# Reads the rolling event log (`<epoch>|<attrib>`, legacy bare-epoch OK) and counts events
# within the window. Pure, file-bound — easy to test.
count_recent_events() {
  local f="$1"
  local now="$2"
  local win="$3"
  local cutoff epoch
  cutoff=$(( now - win ))
  local c=0
  [ -f "$f" ] || { echo 0; return; }
  while IFS= read -r line; do
    [ -z "$line" ] && continue
    epoch="${line%%|*}"
    [ "$epoch" -ge "$cutoff" ] 2>/dev/null && c=$((c+1))
  done < "$f"
  echo "$c"
}

# log_event <log_file> <epoch> [now] [attrib] — appends a tamper event (`<epoch>|<attrib>`,
# attrib defaults UNKNOWN) and prunes anything older than 24h relative to `now` (defaults to
# `epoch` itself when called at the time of a new event). Events older than 24h are NOT appended
# (already-expired events don't pollute the log). One line per moved event. Pure, file-bound.
log_event() {
  local f="$1"
  local epoch="$2"
  local now="${3:-$epoch}"   # when called at event-time, now == epoch
  local attrib="${4:-UNKNOWN}"
  local cutoff=$(( now - 86400 ))
  local lepoch
  [ -d "$(dirname "$f")" ] || return 1
  # Prune: keep only lines whose epoch >= cutoff (last 24h from `now`; legacy bare-epoch lines OK)
  if [ -f "$f" ]; then
    local tmp="${f}.tmp.$$"
    : > "$tmp"
    while IFS= read -r line; do
      [ -z "$line" ] && continue
      lepoch="${line%%|*}"
      [ "$lepoch" -ge "$cutoff" ] 2>/dev/null && printf '%s\n' "$line" >> "$tmp"
    done < "$f"
    mv -f "$tmp" "$f" 2>/dev/null || rm -f "$tmp"
  fi
  # Append the new event — but only if it's not already older than the cutoff (already-expired)
  if [ "$epoch" -ge "$cutoff" ] 2>/dev/null; then
    printf '%s|%s\n' "$epoch" "$attrib" >> "$f"
  fi
}

# write_tamper_state <verdict> <now_epoch> <window> — atomic state write with recency + event count.
# The format is additive: <verdict>|<epoch>|<window>|<recency_label>|<events_24h>|<last_attrib>|<burst>|<cadence>
# Old readers gracefully degrade (an `IFS='|' read -r v t w r e` still works, fields 6-8 ignored;
# window-state's awk field-4 recency read and room-sense's parse are untouched).
write_tamper_state() {
  local verdict="$1" now="$2" window="$3"
  local last_event=""
  [ -f "$EVENT_LOG" ] && last_event="$(tail -n 1 "$EVENT_LOG" 2>/dev/null)"
  local recency; recency="$(classify_recency "$now" "${last_event%%|*}")"
  local events_24h; events_24h="$(count_recent_events "$EVENT_LOG" "$now" 86400)"
  local last_attrib; last_attrib="$(last_event_attrib "$EVENT_LOG")"
  local burst_n; burst_n="$(count_recent_events "$EVENT_LOG" "$now" "$BURST_WINDOW")"
  local burst; burst="$(classify_burst "$burst_n")"
  # Cadence only means something for a BURST (>= 3 events = >= 2 gaps); ISOLATED/NONE → NA.
  local cadence="NA"
  [ "$burst" = "BURST" ] && cadence="$(classify_cadence $(recent_event_epochs "$EVENT_LOG" "$now" "$BURST_WINDOW"))"
  # Store the recency LABEL ONLY (strip classify_recency's "|description" tail) — otherwise the
  # embedded '|' pushes events_24h off field 5, breaking the documented format AND the --status
  # reader. Matches how the live moved/quiet output already uses ${r%%|*}. The attrib, burst and
  # cadence tokens are single-word by construction (classify_* vocabulary), never pipe-embedded.
  printf '%s|%s|%s|%s|%s|%s|%s|%s\n' "$verdict" "$now" "$window" "${recency%%|*}" "$events_24h" "$last_attrib" "$burst" "$cadence" > "$STATE"
}

if [ "${1:-}" = --test ]; then
  command -v ssh >/dev/null 2>&1 || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (no ssh)"; exit 1; }
  command -v mesh-phone-ip >/dev/null 2>&1 || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (no mesh-phone-ip)"; exit 1; }
  _f=0
  # --- classify_tamper() fixtures (OFFLINE, phone-independent — the honest-degraded decision logic) ---
  # MOVED: watch ran (sentinel) + SIGNIFICANT_MOTION fired (values blob present).
  [ "$(classify_tamper 0 '{"SIGNIFICANT_MOTION":{"values":[1.0]}}TAMPER_EOF=0')" = "moved" ] \
    || { echo "  FAIL: sentinel+values should be moved"; _f=1; }
  # QUIET (the recovered verdict): watch ran (sentinel) but nothing fired (no values blob). Was
  # misread unreachable under the old rc=124 logic; now a confident still.
  [ "$(classify_tamper 0 'TAMPER_EOF=124')" = "quiet" ] \
    || { echo "  FAIL: sentinel + no-values should be quiet (watch ran, nothing fired)"; _f=1; }
  # QUIET even when termux cleanup noise rides along on stdout (no values blob → still quiet).
  [ "$(classify_tamper 0 'termux-sensor exitingTAMPER_EOF=124')" = "quiet" ] \
    || { echo "  FAIL: sentinel + cleanup-noise (no values) should be quiet"; _f=1; }
  # UNREACHABLE (ssh hung → outer timeout, rc 124, no sentinel): the honest n/a, never a fake quiet.
  [ "$(classify_tamper 124 '')" = "unreachable" ] \
    || { echo "  FAIL: rc=124 + no sentinel should be unreachable"; _f=1; }
  # UNREACHABLE (ssh refused, rc 255, no sentinel).
  [ "$(classify_tamper 255 '')" = "unreachable" ] \
    || { echo "  FAIL: rc=255 + no sentinel should be unreachable"; _f=1; }
  # REGRESSION GUARD (the core fix): rc=124 WITH a sentinel is NOT unreachable — the watch ran.
  # This is exactly the still-body case the old rc-gated logic collapsed into n/a.
  [ "$(classify_tamper 124 'TAMPER_EOF=124')" = "quiet" ] \
    || { echo "  FAIL: rc=124 + sentinel must be quiet (sentinel gates over rc — the fix)"; _f=1; }
  # moved verdict is rc-agnostic too: sentinel+values → moved regardless of rc.
  [ "$(classify_tamper 124 '{"SIGNIFICANT_MOTION":{"values":[1.0]}}TAMPER_EOF=0')" = "moved" ] \
    || { echo "  FAIL: sentinel+values → moved regardless of rc"; _f=1; }
  # --- classify_recency() fixtures (FALSIFIABLE: change the band thresholds → these flip) ---
  _tnow=1000000000
  # ACTIVE: event just now (0s ago)
  r="$(classify_recency "$_tnow" "$_tnow")"
  echo "$r" | grep -q '^ACTIVE' || { echo "  FAIL recency ACTIVE 0s: got=$r"; _f=1; }
  # ACTIVE: event 4 min 59s ago (just under 5 min threshold)
  r="$(classify_recency "$_tnow" $((_tnow - 299)))"
  echo "$r" | grep -q '^ACTIVE' || { echo "  FAIL recency ACTIVE 299s: got=$r"; _f=1; }
  # RECENT: event 5 min ago (at the boundary)
  r="$(classify_recency "$_tnow" $((_tnow - 300)))"
  echo "$r" | grep -q '^RECENT' || { echo "  FAIL recency RECENT 300s: got=$r"; _f=1; }
  # RECENT: event 29 min ago
  r="$(classify_recency "$_tnow" $((_tnow - 1740)))"
  echo "$r" | grep -q '^RECENT' || { echo "  FAIL recency RECENT 1740s: got=$r"; _f=1; }
  # STALE: event 30 min ago
  r="$(classify_recency "$_tnow" $((_tnow - 1800)))"
  echo "$r" | grep -q '^STALE' || { echo "  FAIL recency STALE 1800s: got=$r"; _f=1; }
  # STALE: event 90 min ago
  r="$(classify_recency "$_tnow" $((_tnow - 5400)))"
  echo "$r" | grep -q '^STALE' || { echo "  FAIL recency STALE 5400s: got=$r"; _f=1; }
  # COLD: event 2 hours ago (at the boundary)
  r="$(classify_recency "$_tnow" $((_tnow - 7200)))"
  echo "$r" | grep -q '^COLD' || { echo "  FAIL recency COLD 7200s: got=$r"; _f=1; }
  # COLD: event 24 hours ago
  r="$(classify_recency "$_tnow" $((_tnow - 86400)))"
  echo "$r" | grep -q '^COLD' || { echo "  FAIL recency COLD 86400s: got=$r"; _f=1; }
  # NEVER: no event (empty)
  r="$(classify_recency "$_tnow" "")"
  [ "$r" = "NEVER|no recorded event" ] || { echo "  FAIL recency NEVER: got=$r"; _f=1; }
  # NEVER: zero event
  r="$(classify_recency "$_tnow" "0")"
  [ "$r" = "NEVER|no recorded event" ] || { echo "  FAIL recency NEVER-zero: got=$r"; _f=1; }
  # --- classify_burst() fixtures (FALSIFIABLE: change BURST_FLOOR/BURST_WINDOW → these flip) ---
  [ "$(classify_burst 0)" = "NONE" ]     || { echo "  FAIL burst 0→NONE: got=$(classify_burst 0)"; _f=1; }
  [ "$(classify_burst 1)" = "ISOLATED" ] || { echo "  FAIL burst 1→ISOLATED: got=$(classify_burst 1)"; _f=1; }
  [ "$(classify_burst 2)" = "ISOLATED" ] || { echo "  FAIL burst 2→ISOLATED (below BURST_FLOOR=3): got=$(classify_burst 2)"; _f=1; }
  [ "$(classify_burst 3)" = "BURST" ]    || { echo "  FAIL burst 3→BURST (at BURST_FLOOR): got=$(classify_burst 3)"; _f=1; }
  [ "$(classify_burst 9)" = "BURST" ]    || { echo "  FAIL burst 9→BURST: got=$(classify_burst 9)"; _f=1; }
  [ "$(classify_burst '')" = "NONE" ]    || { echo "  FAIL burst empty→NONE: got=$(classify_burst '')"; _f=1; }
  # --- classify_cadence() fixtures (FALSIFIABLE: change CADENCE_CV_PERMILLE and even/uneven flip) ---
  # PERIODIC: perfectly even gaps (60s apart) → CoV 0
  [ "$(classify_cadence 1000 1060 1120 1180)" = "PERIODIC" ] || { echo "  FAIL cadence even→PERIODIC: got=$(classify_cadence 1000 1060 1120 1180)"; _f=1; }
  # PERIODIC: near-even gaps (58/61/60) → CoV well under 0.35
  [ "$(classify_cadence 1000 1058 1119 1179)" = "PERIODIC" ] || { echo "  FAIL cadence near-even→PERIODIC: got=$(classify_cadence 1000 1058 1119 1179)"; _f=1; }
  # ERRATIC: wildly uneven gaps (2s then 300s then 5s) → high CoV
  [ "$(classify_cadence 1000 1002 1302 1307)" = "ERRATIC" ] || { echo "  FAIL cadence uneven→ERRATIC: got=$(classify_cadence 1000 1002 1302 1307)"; _f=1; }
  # ERRATIC: rummaging flurry then gap (1,2,3s apart then 200s) → high CoV
  [ "$(classify_cadence 1000 1001 1003 1203)" = "ERRATIC" ] || { echo "  FAIL cadence flurry→ERRATIC: got=$(classify_cadence 1000 1001 1003 1203)"; _f=1; }
  # order-independent: sort makes unsorted input give the same verdict as sorted
  [ "$(classify_cadence 1180 1000 1120 1060)" = "PERIODIC" ] || { echo "  FAIL cadence unsorted→PERIODIC: got=$(classify_cadence 1180 1000 1120 1060)"; _f=1; }
  # NA: fewer than 3 events (< 2 gaps to judge)
  [ "$(classify_cadence 1000 1060)" = "NA" ] || { echo "  FAIL cadence 2-events→NA: got=$(classify_cadence 1000 1060)"; _f=1; }
  [ "$(classify_cadence)" = "NA" ]           || { echo "  FAIL cadence no-events→NA: got=$(classify_cadence)"; _f=1; }
  # NA: coincident timestamps (mean gap 0) → cannot judge, no divide-by-zero
  [ "$(classify_cadence 1000 1000 1000)" = "NA" ] || { echo "  FAIL cadence coincident→NA: got=$(classify_cadence 1000 1000 1000)"; _f=1; }
  # threshold is FALSIFIABLE: a tight cut flips the near-even case to ERRATIC
  [ "$(MESH_TAMPER_CADENCE_CV=5 classify_cadence 1000 1058 1119 1179)" = "ERRATIC" ] || { echo "  FAIL cadence tight-cut→ERRATIC"; _f=1; }
  # --- recent_event_epochs() (file-bound, feeds classify_cadence) ---
  _ced="$(mktemp -d)"; _cef="$_ced/events"
  printf '100|X\n1000|X\n1200|X\n' > "$_cef"   # now=1300 window=600 → cutoff=700; drop 100(<cutoff), keep 1000,1200
  [ "$(recent_event_epochs "$_cef" 1300 600)" = "1000 1200 " ] || { echo "  FAIL recent_event_epochs window filter: got=[$(recent_event_epochs "$_cef" 1300 600)]"; _f=1; }
  [ -z "$(recent_event_epochs "$_ced/absent" 1300 600)" ] || { echo "  FAIL recent_event_epochs missing-log → empty"; _f=1; }
  rm -rf "$_ced"
  # --- count_recent_events() + log_event() fixtures (file-bound, no network) ---
  _etd="$(mktemp -d)"; _ef="$_etd/events"
  log_event "$_ef" 100
  log_event "$_ef" 200
  log_event "$_ef" 300
  c="$(count_recent_events "$_ef" 350 86400)"
  [ "$c" = "3" ] || { echo "  FAIL log_event basic count: expected 3 got=$c"; _f=1; }
  # Pruning: events older than 24h relative to NOW are dropped. Use now=200 so 100/200/300 are
  # within 24h (cutoff=200-86400=-86200), and the new event at epoch -100000 is way before cutoff → dropped.
  log_event "$_ef" -100000 200
  c="$(count_recent_events "$_ef" 200 86400)"
  [ "$c" = "3" ] || { echo "  FAIL log_event pruning: expected 3 after pruning got=$c"; _f=1; }
  # LEGACY interop: a pre-attrib bare-epoch line still counts, prunes, and reads attrib=UNKNOWN.
  printf '400\n' >> "$_ef"
  c="$(count_recent_events "$_ef" 450 86400)"
  [ "$c" = "4" ] || { echo "  FAIL count legacy bare-epoch line: expected 4 got=$c"; _f=1; }
  [ "$(last_event_attrib "$_ef")" = "UNKNOWN" ] || { echo "  FAIL last_event_attrib legacy line → UNKNOWN"; _f=1; }
  log_event "$_ef" 500 500 ATTRIBUTED
  [ "$(last_event_attrib "$_ef")" = "ATTRIBUTED" ] || { echo "  FAIL last_event_attrib new-format line → recorded verdict"; _f=1; }
  grep -q '^500|ATTRIBUTED$' "$_ef" || { echo "  FAIL log_event attrib format: $(tail -1 "$_ef")"; _f=1; }
  [ "$(last_event_attrib "$_etd/absent")" = "NONE" ] || { echo "  FAIL last_event_attrib no log → NONE"; _f=1; }
  rm -rf "$_etd"
  # --- classify_event_attrib() fixtures (event-time attribution vocabulary — mirrors mesh-tamper-attrib;
  #     FALSIFIABLE: change the mapping and these flip) ---
  [ "$(classify_event_attrib ACTIVE_NEAR FAMILIAR)" = "ATTRIBUTED" ] || { echo "  FAIL attrib NEAR+FAMILIAR→ATTRIBUTED"; _f=1; }
  [ "$(classify_event_attrib ACTIVE_FAR FAMILIAR)"  = "ATTRIBUTED" ] || { echo "  FAIL attrib FAR+FAMILIAR→ATTRIBUTED"; _f=1; }
  [ "$(classify_event_attrib ACTIVE_NEAR STRANGER)" = "SUSPECT" ]    || { echo "  FAIL attrib NEAR+STRANGER→SUSPECT"; _f=1; }
  [ "$(classify_event_attrib EMPTY EMPTY)"          = "SUSPECT" ]    || { echo "  FAIL attrib EMPTY→SUSPECT"; _f=1; }
  [ "$(classify_event_attrib APPLIANCE FAMILIAR)"   = "SUSPECT" ]    || { echo "  FAIL attrib APPLIANCE→SUSPECT"; _f=1; }
  [ "$(classify_event_attrib ARRIVING FAMILIAR)"    = "AMBIGUOUS" ]  || { echo "  FAIL attrib ARRIVING→AMBIGUOUS"; _f=1; }
  [ "$(classify_event_attrib ACTIVE_NEAR UNKNOWN)"  = "AMBIGUOUS" ]  || { echo "  FAIL attrib NEAR+unknown-fam→AMBIGUOUS"; _f=1; }
  # honest-degraded: a stale/missing presence input is UNKNOWN, never a fabricated benign
  [ "$(classify_event_attrib STALE STALE)"          = "UNKNOWN" ]    || { echo "  FAIL attrib STALE→UNKNOWN"; _f=1; }
  [ "$(classify_event_attrib MISSING MISSING)"      = "UNKNOWN" ]    || { echo "  FAIL attrib MISSING→UNKNOWN"; _f=1; }
  # --- presence_pf() freshness gate (fixture file — no phone, no live scan) ---
  _pd="$(mktemp -d)"; PRESENCE_STATE="$_pd/presence"
  printf 'personal_presence=ACTIVE_FAR familiarity=FAMILIAR devices=3\n' > "$PRESENCE_STATE"
  [ "$(presence_pf)" = "ACTIVE_FAR|FAMILIAR" ] || { echo "  FAIL presence_pf fresh read: got=$(presence_pf)"; _f=1; }
  touch -d '20 minutes ago' "$PRESENCE_STATE"
  [ "$(presence_pf)" = "STALE|STALE" ] || { echo "  FAIL presence_pf stale (>900s) → STALE: got=$(presence_pf)"; _f=1; }
  rm -f "$PRESENCE_STATE"
  [ "$(presence_pf)" = "MISSING|MISSING" ] || { echo "  FAIL presence_pf missing → MISSING"; _f=1; }
  [ "$(MESH_TAMPER_PRESENCE='EMPTY|EMPTY' presence_pf)" = "EMPTY|EMPTY" ] || { echo "  FAIL presence_pf override hook"; _f=1; }
  rm -rf "$_pd"; PRESENCE_STATE="${MESH_TAMPER_PRESENCE_STATE:-$HOME/.mesh/.presence-state}"
  # --- write_tamper_state() field-format guard: recency's "|desc" must be stripped so events_24h
  #     stays at field 5 (regression for the embedded-pipe bug), last_attrib lands at field 6, and
  #     burst lands at field 7 ---
  _wd="$(mktemp -d)"; STATE="$_wd/state"; EVENT_LOG="$_wd/events"
  log_event "$EVENT_LOG" "$_tnow" "$_tnow" SUSPECT
  write_tamper_state "quiet" "$_tnow" 8
  _nf="$(awk -F'|' '{print NF}' "$STATE")"
  [ "$_nf" = 8 ] || { echo "  FAIL write_tamper_state field count: expected 8 got=$_nf ($(cat "$STATE"))"; _f=1; }
  IFS='|' read -r _wv _wt _ww _wr _we _wa _wb _wc < "$STATE"
  [ "$_we" = 1 ] || { echo "  FAIL write_tamper_state events_24h@field5: expected 1 got=$_we ($(cat "$STATE"))"; _f=1; }
  [ "$_wa" = "SUSPECT" ] || { echo "  FAIL write_tamper_state last_attrib@field6: expected SUSPECT got=$_wa"; _f=1; }
  [ "$_wr" = "ACTIVE" ] || { echo "  FAIL write_tamper_state recency from attrib-format log: expected ACTIVE got=$_wr"; _f=1; }
  case "$_wr" in *'|'*) echo "  FAIL write_tamper_state recency embeds '|': $_wr"; _f=1;; esac
  # single event within BURST_WINDOW → ISOLATED, not BURST (below BURST_FLOOR=3)
  [ "$_wb" = "ISOLATED" ] || { echo "  FAIL write_tamper_state burst@field7: expected ISOLATED got=$_wb"; _f=1; }
  # cadence@field8 is NA when not a BURST (< 3 events → no rhythm to judge)
  [ "$_wc" = "NA" ] || { echo "  FAIL write_tamper_state cadence@field8: expected NA (not BURST) got=$_wc ($(cat "$STATE"))"; _f=1; }
  # a THIRD event within BURST_WINDOW flips the same state write to BURST
  log_event "$EVENT_LOG" "$((_tnow - 60))" "$_tnow" SUSPECT
  log_event "$EVENT_LOG" "$((_tnow - 120))" "$_tnow" SUSPECT
  write_tamper_state "quiet" "$_tnow" 8
  IFS='|' read -r _wv _wt _ww _wr _we _wa _wb _wc < "$STATE"
  [ "$_wb" = "BURST" ] || { echo "  FAIL write_tamper_state burst flips to BURST at 3 events/10min: got=$_wb ($(cat "$STATE"))"; _f=1; }
  # the 3 events are evenly spaced (60s gaps) → cadence PERIODIC now that it's a BURST
  [ "$_wc" = "PERIODIC" ] || { echo "  FAIL write_tamper_state cadence PERIODIC for even BURST: got=$_wc ($(cat "$STATE"))"; _f=1; }
  rm -rf "$_wd"; unset STATE EVENT_LOG; STATE="$HOME/.mesh/.tamper-state"; EVENT_LOG="$HOME/.mesh/.tamper-events"
  # --- ambient_context() mapping + tamper_sev_render() severity gate (discover/tamper-rhythm-severity) ---
  [ "$(MESH_TAMPER_AMBIENT=QUIET ambient_context)"       = QUIET ]   || { echo "  FAIL ambient QUIET→QUIET"; _f=1; }
  [ "$(MESH_TAMPER_AMBIENT=NIGHT-QUIET ambient_context)" = QUIET ]   || { echo "  FAIL ambient NIGHT-QUIET→QUIET"; _f=1; }
  [ "$(MESH_TAMPER_AMBIENT=MODERATE ambient_context)"    = ACTIVE ]  || { echo "  FAIL ambient MODERATE→ACTIVE"; _f=1; }
  [ "$(MESH_TAMPER_AMBIENT=ACTIVE ambient_context)"      = ACTIVE ]  || { echo "  FAIL ambient ACTIVE→ACTIVE"; _f=1; }
  [ "$(MESH_TAMPER_AMBIENT=DATA-STALE ambient_context)"  = UNKNOWN ] || { echo "  FAIL ambient DATA-STALE→UNKNOWN"; _f=1; }
  # fail-open honest-fusion: ambient-clock unreachable (PATH-stubbed) → UNKNOWN, never a faked all-clear
  [ "$(PATH=/nonexistent ambient_context)"               = UNKNOWN ] || { echo "  FAIL ambient fail-open (tool absent)→UNKNOWN"; _f=1; }
  # severity render: QUIET → ANOMALOUS; ACTIVE → routine; UNKNOWN → default phrasing (marker + fields intact)
  _m="$(tamper_sev_render QUIET 8 3 ATTRIBUTED)";   { echo "$_m" | grep -q '\[body-moved\]' && echo "$_m" | grep -q 'ANOMALOUS'; } || { echo "  FAIL sev QUIET→anomalous: $_m"; _f=1; }
  _m="$(tamper_sev_render ACTIVE 8 3 ATTRIBUTED)";  echo "$_m" | grep -q 'routine' || { echo "  FAIL sev ACTIVE→routine: $_m"; _f=1; }
  _m="$(tamper_sev_render UNKNOWN 8 3 SUSPECT)"; { echo "$_m" | grep -q '\[body-moved\] SIGNIFICANT_MOTION' && echo "$_m" | grep -q 'recency=ACTIVE events_24h=3'; } || { echo "  FAIL sev UNKNOWN→default unchanged: $_m"; _f=1; }
  # the moved line carries the event-time attribution; omitted attrib degrades to UNKNOWN, never absent
  echo "$_m" | grep -q 'attrib=SUSPECT' || { echo "  FAIL sev render attrib field: $_m"; _f=1; }
  _m="$(tamper_sev_render ACTIVE 8 3)"; echo "$_m" | grep -q 'attrib=UNKNOWN' || { echo "  FAIL sev render attrib default UNKNOWN: $_m"; _f=1; }
  # burst param: omitted → burst=NONE, no BURST note (byte-compatible w/ pre-burst 4-arg callers)
  echo "$_m" | grep -q 'burst=NONE' || { echo "  FAIL sev render burst default NONE: $_m"; _f=1; }
  echo "$_m" | grep -qv 'sustained handling' || { echo "  FAIL sev render should not note burst when NONE: $_m"; _f=1; }
  # BURST fires its own note regardless of ambient (self-evidencing, not household-clock-gated)
  _m="$(tamper_sev_render UNKNOWN 8 5 ATTRIBUTED BURST)"
  { echo "$_m" | grep -q 'burst=BURST' && echo "$_m" | grep -q 'sustained handling'; } \
    || { echo "  FAIL sev render BURST note under UNKNOWN ambient: $_m"; _f=1; }
  # BURST stacks with an ANOMALOUS quiet-hour read — both notes present, strongest combined severity
  _m="$(tamper_sev_render QUIET 8 5 SUSPECT BURST)"
  { echo "$_m" | grep -q 'ANOMALOUS' && echo "$_m" | grep -q 'sustained handling'; } \
    || { echo "  FAIL sev render BURST+QUIET should stack both notes: $_m"; _f=1; }
  # ISOLATED: no burst note (only BURST triggers the extra phrase)
  _m="$(tamper_sev_render ACTIVE 8 1 ATTRIBUTED ISOLATED)"
  echo "$_m" | grep -q 'burst=ISOLATED' || { echo "  FAIL sev render burst=ISOLATED field: $_m"; _f=1; }
  echo "$_m" | grep -qv 'sustained handling' || { echo "  FAIL sev render should not note burst when ISOLATED: $_m"; _f=1; }
  # cadence field is always emitted; default NA when omitted (byte-compatible with 5-arg callers)
  _m="$(tamper_sev_render ACTIVE 8 5 ATTRIBUTED BURST)"; echo "$_m" | grep -q 'cadence=NA' || { echo "  FAIL sev render cadence default NA: $_m"; _f=1; }
  # a PERIODIC BURST notes the mechanical/periodic down-rank; an ERRATIC BURST notes human-handling
  _m="$(tamper_sev_render QUIET 8 5 SUSPECT BURST PERIODIC)"
  { echo "$_m" | grep -q 'cadence=PERIODIC' && echo "$_m" | grep -q 'mechanical/periodic'; } || { echo "  FAIL sev render PERIODIC note: $_m"; _f=1; }
  _m="$(tamper_sev_render QUIET 8 5 SUSPECT BURST ERRATIC)"
  { echo "$_m" | grep -q 'cadence=ERRATIC' && echo "$_m" | grep -q 'human handling'; } || { echo "  FAIL sev render ERRATIC note: $_m"; _f=1; }
  # cadence note only rides on a BURST — an ISOLATED/omitted burst never prints the cadence phrase
  _m="$(tamper_sev_render ACTIVE 8 1 ATTRIBUTED ISOLATED PERIODIC)"
  echo "$_m" | grep -qv 'mechanical/periodic' || { echo "  FAIL sev render cadence note must not ride on ISOLATED: $_m"; _f=1; }
  [ "$_f" = 0 ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (classify_tamper + classify_recency + classify_burst + classify_cadence + recent_epochs + log_event + event-attrib + presence-pf + write_state + ambient-context + tamper-sev)"; exit 1; }
  # --- phone reachability gate (unchanged): real SSH probe, not mesh-phone-ip's cached IP ---
  pip="$(mesh-phone-ip 2>/dev/null)" || { echo "smoke-test: n/a (phone unreachable — all IPs tried); classify-logic ok"; exit 2; }
  timeout 10 ssh -p "${PHONE_SSH_PORT:-8022}" -o BatchMode=yes -o ConnectTimeout=6 \
    -o StrictHostKeyChecking=accept-new "${PHONE_USER:-u0_a380}@$pip" 'command -v termux-sensor' </dev/null >/dev/null 2>&1 \
    || { echo "smoke-test: n/a (phone unreachable or no termux-sensor); classify-logic ok"; exit 2; }
  echo "smoke-test: ok (classify: 7 + recency: 9 + burst: 6 + cadence: 9 + recent-epochs: 2 + log: 7 + event-attrib: 9 + presence-pf: 4 + write_state: 9 + ambient-context: 6 + tamper-sev: 15 = 83 assertions + reachability)"; exit 0
fi

window=8; edge=0; status=0
while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
  case "$1" in
    --window) window="${2:-8}"; shift;;
    --edge) edge=1;;
    --status) status=1;;
  esac; shift
done

# --status: non-blocking cached read of the last disturbance verdict + age (no phone watch, no block).
# Lets a consumer or reflex-health check tamper liveness WITHOUT paying the ~window-second watch,
# and makes the sense OBSERVABLE (it was the only body sense with no state artifact). Honest: a stale
# or absent state reads as such — never a fabricated "quiet" (the unreachable branch does not write).
if [ "$status" = 1 ]; then
  if [ -f "$STATE" ]; then
    IFS='|' read -r _v _t _w _r _e _a _b _c < "$STATE" 2>/dev/null
    _age=$(( $(date +%s) - ${_t:-0} ))
    echo "tamper: ${_v:-unknown} (${_age}s ago, window=${_w:-?}, recency=${_r:-?}, events_24h=${_e:-?}, last_attrib=${_a:-?}, burst=${_b:-NONE}, cadence=${_c:-NA})"
  else
    echo "tamper: no cached state yet (never produced)"
  fi
  exit 0
fi

pip="$(mesh-phone-ip 2>/dev/null)" || { 
  # Unreachable: write OFFLINE state (mesh-state-touch) so the dashboard sees a recent mark
  # rather than an ancient mtime. The previous code's "no state write on unreachable" was the
  # exact hollow-sense pattern (no false-fail via mtime decay) — the same class of fix applied
  # to mesh-cooscillate and mesh-correlate. Recency stays whatever it was before the gap.
  NOW=$(date +%s)
  printf 'OFFLINE|%s|%s|%s|%s|%s|%s|%s\n' "$NOW" "$window" "OFFLINE" "0" "$(last_event_attrib "$EVENT_LOG")" "NONE" "NA" > "$STATE"   # 8 clean fields (no embedded '|')
  mesh-state-touch "$STATE"
  echo "phone unreachable (all IPs tried) — no tamper sense" >&2
  exit 2
}

# Watch SIGNIFICANT_MOTION for the window. It BLOCKS until the HAL fires the trigger, then returns
# a values blob; if nothing fires, the inner timeout kills it (rc 124) and we get no values blob.
# The trailing `echo TAMPER_EOF=$?` is the PROOF-THE-WATCH-RAN sentinel: it prints ONLY if the ssh
# session ran the remote shell to completion (phone reachable), disambiguating a still body (watch
# ran, nothing fired → quiet) from a dead link (no sentinel → unreachable). \$? is evaluated remotely.
run_watch() {
  local w="$1" inner
  inner=$(( w > 2 ? w - 1 : 1 ))
  timeout $(( w + 4 )) ssh -p "${PHONE_SSH_PORT:-8022}" -o BatchMode=yes -o ConnectTimeout=6 \
    -o StrictHostKeyChecking=accept-new "${PHONE_USER:-u0_a380}@$pip" \
    "timeout $inner termux-sensor -s SIGNIFICANT_MOTION -n 1 2>/dev/null; echo TAMPER_EOF=\$?" </dev/null 2>/dev/null
}

out="$(run_watch "$window")"; rc=$?
verdict="$(classify_tamper "$rc" "$out")"
if [ "$verdict" = "unreachable" ]; then
  # Doze-window first-SSH-drop (retry-once standard, 5c47ea1): the first connect to a dozing phone
  # often dies in handshake — the outer timeout fires before the sentinel — while WAKING the link,
  # so a second attempt right after completes (live 2026-07-07: two false OFFLINEs from a phone that
  # answered a battery probe seconds later, then a 7.5s clean watch). Write fresh OFFLINE state
  # BEFORE retrying so the dashboard's mtime-freshness survives even if a caller's outer `timeout 15`
  # kills the retry mid-flight; a completed retry overwrites it with the recovered real verdict.
  NOW=$(date +%s)
  write_tamper_state "OFFLINE" "$NOW" "$window"
  mesh-state-touch "$STATE"
  out="$(run_watch "$window")"; rc=$?
  verdict="$(classify_tamper "$rc" "$out")"
fi
NOW=$(date +%s)
case "$verdict" in
  moved)
    # Event-time attribution: read the cached presence familiarity NOW (the moment the HAL fired) —
    # the best evidence of WHO for this event, recorded with it so the recency window keeps it.
    _pf="$(presence_pf)"
    _attrib="$(classify_event_attrib "${_pf%%|*}" "${_pf#*|}")"
    log_event "$EVENT_LOG" "$NOW" "$NOW" "$_attrib"
    write_tamper_state "moved" "$NOW" "$window"
    # Output: recency band + event count (consumer contract) PLUS ambient-gated disturbance severity
    # PLUS burst context — a live disturbance while the household is QUIET is anomalous; during
    # activity it's routine; a BURST (multiple fires packed into BURST_WINDOW) is sustained handling
    # regardless of ambient, and stacks with an ANOMALOUS quiet-hour read.
    _e="$(count_recent_events "$EVENT_LOG" "$NOW" 86400)"
    _bn="$(count_recent_events "$EVENT_LOG" "$NOW" "$BURST_WINDOW")"
    _burst="$(classify_burst "$_bn")"
    _cadence="NA"; [ "$_burst" = "BURST" ] && _cadence="$(classify_cadence $(recent_event_epochs "$EVENT_LOG" "$NOW" "$BURST_WINDOW"))"
    tamper_sev_render "$(ambient_context)" "$window" "$_e" "$_attrib" "$_burst" "$_cadence"
    exit 0 ;;
  quiet)
    write_tamper_state "quiet" "$NOW" "$window"
    [ "$edge" = 1 ] && exit 0   # quiet: silent in edge mode
    # Surface the recency band in the human-readable line so consumers can see "last event was 12m ago"
    _last_event=""
    [ -f "$EVENT_LOG" ] && _last_event="$(tail -n 1 "$EVENT_LOG" 2>/dev/null)"
    _recency="$(classify_recency "$NOW" "${_last_event%%|*}")"
    _e="$(count_recent_events "$EVENT_LOG" "$NOW" 86400)"
    _bn="$(count_recent_events "$EVENT_LOG" "$NOW" "$BURST_WINDOW")"
    _burst="$(classify_burst "$_bn")"
    _cadence="NA"; [ "$_burst" = "BURST" ] && _cadence="$(classify_cadence $(recent_event_epochs "$EVENT_LOG" "$NOW" "$BURST_WINDOW"))"
    printf '[body-quiet] no significant motion in %ss — body undisturbed (recency=%s events_24h=%s last_attrib=%s burst=%s cadence=%s)\n' \
      "$window" "${_recency%%|*}" "$_e" "$(last_event_attrib "$EVENT_LOG")" "$_burst" "$_cadence"
    exit 0 ;;
  *)
    # Unreachable: write OFFLINE state (honest-degraded, never faked) so the dashboard sees a fresh mtime
    write_tamper_state "OFFLINE" "$NOW" "$window"
    mesh-state-touch "$STATE"
    echo "phone unreachable / watch did not run — tamper read n/a (NOT 'quiet')" >&2
    exit 2 ;;
esac
